  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Passive with dative",
     "texto": [
      "In German there are verbs with dative (**helfen**, **danken**, **gratulieren**). In the passive the dative is kept and the sentence begins with it, with **es** or without a real subject: **Mir wurde geholfen** (I was helped). Literally: \"to me it was helped\".",
      "With \"es\" in front, it is used to introduce the sentence: **Es wurde mir geholfen** (I was helped; it was helped to me)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Dative verbs in the passive",
     "texto": [
      "With intransitive verbs that do not take an accusative complement, such as **helfen**, **danken** and **gratulieren**, the passive is usually impersonal and keeps the dative: **Mir wurde gedankt** (I was thanked), **Ihm wurde gratuliert** (he was congratulated). The rule does not apply to all dative verbs: if the verb also takes an accusative object, that object can become the subject of the passive: **Der Chef gab mir den Schlüssel** → **Der Schlüssel wurde mir gegeben** (the boss gave me the key → the key was given to me). Beware of the false friend: in German **gedankt werden** means \"to receive thanks\"."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
